            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why do we need this differentiation?  Aren't most SPI flashes generic and you don't need to know if it is a host or something else?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "KyleHsieh.Quanta",
                        "email": "Kyle.Hsieh@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "KyleHsieh171"
                    },
                    "message": "Previously, using \"BIOS\" wasn't intuitive enough, so we shifted towards SPI flash terminology. However, since we now have multiple SPI devices, we use specific types like HOSTSPIFlash and IntelSPIFlash to distinguish them.\n\nThe core reason is that each SPI device requires a different method to retrieve its firmware version. The software update daemon uses this Type field to determine which specific getVersion() implementation to invoke. Without this differentiation, the daemon wouldn't know how to correctly query and populate the software version on D-Bus."
                }
            ],
